% The Vinberg diagram of (10,8,0)_1 = U + E_8(2): nine roots of square -4
% (black) forming T_{2,3,6} in Bourbaki's numbering of E_9, and one root 10 of
% square -2 (white) joined to the end 9 of the long arm with weight 4. It is
% also the Vinberg diagram of (10,10,1)_1 = U(2) + A_1^8, and the Coxeter
% diagram of the 0-cusp eta_2 of F_{En,2} (Sterk's diagram II), whose lattice
% is eta_2^perp/eta_2 = (10,8,0)_1 (objects/bb-cusps/fen2): the Sterk_2
% roots of dzack_research.preamble.sterk, Sterk.sterk_roots() (8 of norm -2,
% the rest -4; 0..7 simple chain, 2 -- 9 simple, 7 -- 8 weight 4) form this
% weighted graph, root i of Sterk_2 being vertex 1, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10, 2
% for i = 0..9 (networkx isomorphism with marks and weights as colours; the
% E~_8 and B~_8 parabolics correspond), so W_2 is the full reflection group
% W(U + E_8(2)).
% Computed: the output of Vinberg's algorithm on the lattice of the
% 2-elementary table in the research preamble
% (dzack_research.preamble.catalogue._TWO_ELEMENTARY_RECIPES), run with the
% engine the preamble registers for "vinberg_root_enumeration" (vinal,
% Bogachev-Perepechko); the search completed. Edge weights are read from the
% root Gram matrix, cos(pi/m) = |h_i.h_j| / sqrt(h_i^2 h_j^2), and the maximal
% parabolic subdiagrams are all vertex sets on which minus the Gram matrix is
% positive semidefinite with one kernel direction per component and rank
% rk(L) - 2.
% Maximal parabolic subdiagrams (`maximal parabolic=<name>`):
% E8 = E~_8 = {1..9}, B8 = B~_8 = {2..10}.
% Roots -1..-10, labelled by their index.
\coxeterlabels{index}
\coxeterEdiagram{9}{black}
\coxeterroot{10}{white}{8,0}{below}
\coxeteredges{4}{(-9) -- (-10)}
\coxeterparabolic{E8}{{1,3,4,5,6,7,8,9},{2,4}}
\coxeterparabolic{B8}{{3,4,5,6,7,8,9,10},{2,4}}
